  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Unseasonably warm today, with temperatures 10-15 degrees above\n   normal. \n\n-  Cold front Friday afternoon brings strong west winds, choppy\n   lakes, and elevated fire weather concerns.\n\n-  Cutoff low this weekend brings rain showers to Northwest\n   Montana and snow to high peaks of Glacier National Park.\n\nThe Northern Rockies will be under the influence of a southwesterly\nflow pattern aloft today, with temperatures running 10-15 degrees\nabove normal. An upper level ridge axis along and east of the\ndivide will lead to dry conditions, with plenty of sunshine.\nSoutherly winds will develop by the afternoon, as a low pressure\nsystem reaches the Oregon coastline. The strongest gusts (20-30\nmph) will be felt across the higher elevations and southern Idaho\nCounty, ID. \n\nA significant change arrives Friday as a dry cold front \napproaches from the west, associated with the aforementioned low \npressure system. The front will bring gusty to locally strong \nwinds across western Montana, crossing the US-93 corridor between\n400-700 PM MDT. Most locations will see peak wind gusts of 30-40 \nmph, with localized gusts of 45 mph or greater across the \nBitterroot Valley and in southwest Montana. The Bitterroot Valley\nin particular will experience localized wind channeling effects \nas stronger gusts (45+ mph) descent from narrow west-east\norientated canyons towards the valley floor. These stronger gusts\nmay lead to localized power outages Friday evening. Higher gusts \nof 45-60 mph will be felt across mountain ridgetops and the \nhigher peaks Friday evening. Furthermore, choppy conditions on \nregional lakes and elevated fire weather conditions are expected. \nA brief line of high-based showers will accompany the front, \nalong and north of the I-90 corridor, but little to any \nprecipitation is expected.\n\nA shortwave trough will track along the USA-Canada border Friday\nnight into Saturday morning, focusing shower activity across \nnorthwest Montana and in Clearwater County, Idaho. Snow levels \nwill lower to around 6,000 feet by Saturday morning. Snow showers\nwill bring localized snow accumulations to mountain ranges in \nnorthwest Montana, and the higher terrain of Glacier National \nPark, including Logan Pass. This system will bring a significant \nweather change, increasing the risk of hypothermia from colder \ntemperatures and snowfall following recent warm and dry \nconditions. Seasonably cool and breezy conditions will persist \nthrough Sunday.",
